The R6 byte-diff found ten rewrite-only bugs, all fixed at the source:

- The rewrite lexer accumulated integer literals in signed `i64`, so
  `18446744073709551615u64` trapped halfway through the scan. It now
  accumulates in `u64` and reinterprets the same-width bits.
- A concrete `methods Box<u8>` block passed already-resolved type *ids* to
  `instantiate_type`, which expected type-AST *handles*. It therefore
  resolved to the wrong instance (`Box<i32>`), and its method was emitted
  under the wrong symbol.
- Generic instances shared the template body. Its expression types and call
  targets therefore belonged to whichever instance was checked first. Each
  instance now owns a deep-cloned, substituted, re-checked body.
- Instance names used raw display names and joined arguments with one `_`.
  The backend needs harmony-base's exact legal-symbol mangling
  (`first__Rstr`, `Pair__i32__u8`).
- Template-only placeholder instances (`Box__T`, `Box__Box__T`) leaked into
  the emitted type table. They are now excluded, recursively.
- Range bounds were not stamped as `isize`, and a range `for` did not stamp
  its element type. The backend consequently widened i32 bounds at each use
  and lowered `usize`/`u8` ranges at i32 width.
- The module loader discarded syntax diagnostics from imported files, making
  malformed modules look empty, and it registered a module before its imports.
  Diagnostics now survive, and depth-first merge order matches harmony-base
  (function-definition order is observable in the IR).
- Generic functions imported under a qualified name (`geometry::id`) were not
  instantiated. `ordinary_call` now recognizes qualified generic templates.
- Struct fields were added as their types resolved, so a generic field's
  instance members interleaved with the enclosing struct's members. All field
  types now resolve first, then all members are added, matching harmony-base.

