  needed because the parallel clang path produces a different (smaller,
  non-cross-module-inlined) binary from a single clang invocation; every
  canonical stage uses the same path.
- [x] harmony-base's LLVM string-literal pool now grows past 1024 entries.
  The old fixed table silently aliased later literals to `@.str.0`, which
  corrupted the compiler's own `VERSION` path once the driver grew large.
  Regression `tests/harmony/string-pool.sh`.
- [x] **Deterministic multi-core lowering by default.** `emit.hr` builds the
  exact sequential function order (free functions, non-generic methods, then
  monomorphized instances), then forks `--jobs` workers (default: three per CPU
  in this process's affinity mask, via `sched_getaffinity(2)`, capped at 32 and
  then by `MemAvailable` from `/proc/meminfo`, ~1 GiB per worker) over
  contiguous slices. Explicit job counts obey the same memory cap, so a 4 GiB
  box never forks a swap storm. Workers inherit the checked program through
  copy-on-write and write their IR chunk to a private `/tmp` file; the parent
  joins in spawn order and concatenates the chunks. Small programs stay
  sequential. `--jobs=1` / `--sequential` are the explicit single-core paths.
  `tests/harmony-final/parallel.sh` diffs the safe job count against
  harmony-base, traces actual forks, and asserts that even `--jobs=8` respects
  the memory cap; it uses serial self-builds on small-memory machines.
- [x] **R8 native LLVM-C + embedded LLD backend.** `build`/`run` link against
  `libLLVM` through `extern "C"` bindings in `library/std/llvm.hr`. The shim
  `library/runtime/llvmc.cpp` parses the deterministic IR text, runs LLVM's
  `default<O2>` pipeline, emits a relocatable object, and links with LLD in
  process. Forked workers do the parse/optimise/emit step per chunk; the
  parent links in stable order. LLD discovers the host crt/libc paths itself,
  so there is no generated `host-link.txt` and no external tool. A self
  rebuild executes no child process at all. `--backend=clang` keeps the
  previous path for debugging and `emit-llvm` remains byte-identical.
  `parallel.sh` checks both backends and traces self rebuilds for external
  executions. The runtime directory moved from `bootstrap/runtime/` to
  `library/runtime/`; `hostmain.c` provides the hosted entry point that
  initialises libc before libLLVM is used.
  - Module checking and monomorphization themselves remain sequential: they
    mutate one shared type table whose ids and first-instantiation order are
    observable in the IR. Splitting them safely needs an explicit dependency
    graph and stable id assignment (M2+/borrowck work); doing it by merging
    worker tables ad hoc would break the byte-identical IR gate. The final
    compiler is nevertheless multi-core by default on every non-trivial
    `build`/`run`/`emit-llvm`.
- [x] Fixed a rewrite checker quirk found by the new source: `stamp_lits`
  recursed into typed children and stamped a constant array index with the
  enclosing expression's type (`a[80] as usize` widened at i64 instead of
