### V1 batch 1 — final compiler only [/]

- [x] **Structured `unchecked` reasons.** `unchecked(tag, "free text")`
  is accepted for both blocks and `unchecked fn`. The tag is stored in the
  node's `sfx` slot; `audit` prints `"tag"` in JSON and prefixes the tag in
  pretty output only when present, so the v0 report is byte-identical.
- [x] **Guarded `switch` arms.** `case P if (expr): { ... }` stores the guard
  in the arm's `c` slot. The checker binds the pattern first, then requires
  the guard to be `bool`; codegen emits a conditional branch from the arm
  label to the arm body or the next arm's test. A non-bool guard is
  `TYPE-03`.
  - Exhaustiveness follows §20.3 item 2: a guarded arm counts only when its
    pattern alone is irrefutable for that variant (`A(x)`, bare `A`), so
    `A(5) if (...)` still needs a `default`. Scalar switches always need a
    `default`.
- [x] **`Shared<T>` and `Weak<T>`.** `library/std/shared.hr` implements a
  non-atomic control block (`strong`, `weak`, `value`) over `heap`. `make`,
  `clone`, `get`, `strong_count`, `weak_count`, `downgrade`, and explicit
  `release` cover the strong side; `upgrade -> Maybe<Shared<T>>`,
  `is_expired`, and `release` cover the weak side. The last strong owner
  destroys the value and drops the implicit weak count; the last weak owner
  frees the block. Drop glue is not implemented yet, so `release` is visible
  at the call site — the "visibly costly" part of §20.3 item 3.
- [x] **Threads, joined and detached.** `library/std/thread.hr` exports
  `thread::spawn`, `Thread::join` and `Thread::detach` over runtime hooks in
  `library/runtime/rt.c`. Each thread is a `CLONE_THREAD` task with an mmap'd
  stack; a control block holds `refs`, a futex `done` word and the entry's
  `i64` result. Whichever side drops the last reference frees the control
  block; a peer also unmaps the stack, while a thread dropping its own last
  reference leaves the stack for process exit (it cannot unmap the page it is
  running on), so detached threads need no reaper. The bump allocator is guarded by
  an atomic spinlock; process-level exits use `SYS_exit_group` while a thread
  ends with `SYS_exit`. The runtime needs no libc or pthread, so both the
  native and `--backend=clang` links still work.
  - `Share<T>::as_raw()` returns an **opaque, non-exported `ThreadArg`**, the
    capability marker `spawn` requires. A caller cannot fabricate one for a
    pointer it does not own (a raw pointer argument is `TYPE-01`).
  - `fnptr(f)` (node `ival` 18) is the final-only intrinsic for an **opaque**
    function address used by thread entries; the checker stores the declaration
    handle in `b` and codegen emits `@harmony__f`. Typed, first-class function
    pointers are a separate v1 item (below); `fnptr` does not produce one.
- [x] **Early exit on failure via an `else` clause (§20.3 item 5, redesigned).**
  The `try` keyword was removed entirely. A new `AST_ELSE` node (kind 43)
  attaches to `let`, plain `=` assignment and expression statements; the
  parser accepts `else return;`, `else return expr;`, `else continue;`,
  `else break;`, `else (e) { ... };`, `else { ... };` and a terminal call such
  as `else trap("...");`. The scrutinee must be `Maybe` or `Outcome`; the
  checker binds the success payload to the target, scopes `(e)` to the clause
  block, requires an exit on every path (FLOW-01), and checks forwarding
  shape/error types (FLOW-02/03). Codegen evaluates the scrutinee once,
  splits on the failure tag, rebuilds `Bad(e)`/`Empty` in `%sret` on
  forwarding, and binds the success payload on the `else.ok` path.
- [x] **harmony-base fix:** `ty_find_member` now bounds-checks the flat member
  table before reading a field name. A stale/primitive type id in a struct
  initializer used to read past the table and segfault the compiler; it now
  reports "no such member" like any other unknown field.
- [x] **Named enum payload fields and struct patterns (§20.3 item 6).**
  `parse_enum` accepts `Variant { name: Type, ... }`: field types stay in the
  variant's `kids` (so positional construction still works) and the names in
  `kids2`, which `fill_enum`/`instantiate_type` copy onto the payload struct.
  `Enum::Variant { .name = value }` constructs one (the checker stamps the
  variant index in `ival`; codegen writes the tag and named payload fields).
  A new `AST_PAT_STRUCT`/`AST_PAT_FIELD` pair parses `case Point { x: 0, y: y }:`
  with optional shorthand binds; the checker resolves fields by name, and
  codegen ANDs literal-field tests with the variant tag test (for enums) and
  binds the remaining fields. Unknown fields are `TYPE-02`; a struct pattern
  on a non-struct/enum subject is `TYPE-01`.
- [x] **First-class function pointers (§20.3 item 7).** A `fn(Params) -> Ret`
      type node (`AST_TYPE_FN`, kind 26) resolves to a structural `CTY_FN`
      entry: the return type in `a`, parameter types in the member list, and
      a pointer-sized, `copy` layout. A declared function name used as a value
      stamps its declaration in `target` and lowers to its LLVM global; the
      signature must match the expected type (`TYPE-01`). Calls through a
      function-pointer local, parameter, field or array element check arity
      (`TYPE-04`) and argument types (`TYPE-01`); codegen emits an indirect
      `call ptr` with the same sret conventions as a direct call. Qualified
      module functions work as values too. `fnptr(name)` remains the separate
      opaque-address intrinsic for threads. New gates: `fnptr-basic`,
      `fnptr-field`, `fnptr-dispatch`, `fnptr-qualified` plus three type
      rejections and two syntax/name rejections.
- [x] **User-defined abilities (§20.3 item 10).** `abilities Name { fn sig; ... }`
      declares required method signatures (`AST_ABILITIES`, kind 27). The
      keyword is lexed only by the final compiler, bounds accept any name in
      addition to `copy`/`eq`/`ord`, and `find_method` resolves a requirement
      on a bounded generic parameter during the template check. A structural
      check at each generic instantiation (`instantiate_fn` and
      `instantiate_type`) verifies that the concrete type has matching methods
      (`ABLT-01`); unknown or duplicate ability names are `ABLT-02`. A foreign
      `trait` block in item position produces the documented `NAME-02` hint.
      No codegen changes: abilities are compile-time only, and template bodies
      are never emitted. New gates: `abilities-basic`, `abilities-bound-mix`,
      `abilities-struct` plus five rejections.
- [ ] No v1 compiler features remain. Channels (§19.8.8) are future work; the
  current primitive is a `Share<T>` box plus `join`.
- [ ] **Scoped AST macros (future): design documented, not implemented.**
  docs/harmony.md §20.5 records the planned model: expansion at stage 3.5,
  `#name(...)` calls with `#` and `...` as reserved tokens, contextual
  `expr`/`code`/`block`/`type`/`ident` parameter kinds, hygienic scopes with
  provenance for caller-origin `return`/`break`/`continue`, variadic packs,
  and `MACRO-01..04`. No compiler stage expands macros today; `macro` stays
  reserved and no v1 test covers it.

`tests/harmony-final/v1.sh` runs the batch: twenty-five accepted programs,
twenty-one rejections (guards, `fnptr`, the thread capability, four `else`/FLOW
checks, three field/pattern checks, five function-pointer checks and five
ability checks), and both audit formats.
harmony-base is unchanged apart from the `ty_find_member` bounds guard.

## What harmony-base must still grow (feed it back)

Any gap found while writing `harmony` is fixed in `harmony-base` first, with a
regression test, then used. Bugs found this way already (2026-11, while
starting the rewrite):

- `for (i : 0..n)` took the element type from the lower bound only, so an
  untyped `0` with `n: usize` emitted mixed-width IR. Fixed; regressions
  `for-range-usize`, `for-range-bound-mismatch`.
- `dealloc(p, n)` without `<T>` was resolved as a variable
  ("no `dealloc` in scope") instead of the builtin; it now infers the element
  type from the pointer, which is how §17.8 writes it. Regression
  `dealloc-inferred`.
- `alloc(n)` without `<T>` still cannot infer (only the result names the
  element), so it now says exactly that instead of blaming an unknown name.
  Regression `alloc-missing-targ`.
- `Raw::null()` without `<T>` (the §17.8 form) was also resolved as a name.
  It now infers from the expected type, and struct-literal *fields* pass
  their type down as the expected type (`ck_expr_struct_init` used to check
  the value before looking the field up). Regression
  `struct-field-expected-ty`.
- `const A: usize = 8;` (any non-`i32` const with a literal initializer) was
  a TYPE-01 stop: the checker defaulted the literal to `i32` *before* asking
  whether it was coercible. Regression `const-literal-adopts-type`.
- A `const` exported from an imported module could not be used at all. The
  loader renames it to `mod::NAME`, and both the checker and the backend
  treated every `::` name as an *enum path*, so `mod::NAME` was NAME-01
  "the name before `::` must be an enum or module". Both now consult the
  symbol table first. Regression `module-const`.
- A free function and a method with the same name in one module collided:
  the loader renamed the method's own fn node too, so the method table was
  keyed by `mod::name` and lookups by the bare name failed (TYPE-02 "no such
  field"). This alone made `library/core/str.hr` unusable (`len(&str)` and
  `String::len`). Regression `method-shadow`.
- A `u64` literal ≥ 2^63 emitted **no operand**: the emitter's decimal loop
  was `while (v > 0)` and the lexer stores the value in a signed 64-bit
  `int`, so 18446744073709551615 was negative and produced an empty string
  (`i64 ` with nothing after it). Unsigned literals now print their unsigned
  decimal. Regression `unsigned-large-literal`.
- Unsigned `%` used `srem`: the remainder branch returned before the
  unsigned check, so `u64 % u64` gave the *signed* answer (`-1` for the max
  value). It now uses `urem` (matching the existing `udiv`). Same regression.
- The `str` lang item (the one §19.8.9 reserves) was missing: nothing could
  build a `&str` from owned bytes, so a compiler could not synthesize a
  symbol name or a formatted number. `str::from_parts(p, len)` is now a
  builtin (`unchecked`, since the pointer is unchecked). It is what
  `library/core/str.hr`'s `String::as_str` uses.
- The module loader skipped type arguments on a bare generic path
  (`Vec<N>::make` stores `N` in the path's `kids`): an unconditional early
  return for `AST_PATH` left the argument unqualified, so importing a module
  that used one of its own types as a generic argument produced NAME-01 "no
  type named `N`" and a `Vec__X` instance. Bare paths now walk their kids
  (qualified paths still leave their segments alone). Regression
  `module-generic-path`.
- `ll_addr` had no `AST_CALL` case, so a field access on a call result
  (`make(x).field`) emitted a GEP on `null` -- the call itself was omitted.
  Small structs were silently UB that clang folded into a constant (the
  test used to pass by accident); larger ones faulted. Aggregate-returning
  calls now lower through `ll_expr` and yield the sret result address.
  Regression `field-call-result`.
- Comparing two enum values with `==`/`!=` emitted invalid IR: an enum is
  address-represented, so the operands reached the integer compare as
  `ptr`/address values (`icmp eq %enum.E %addr, ...`), which clang rejects.
  Equality is tag equality; both sides are now loaded as the i32 tag first.
  Found because the rewrite's parser compares token kinds everywhere.
  Regression `enum-compare`.
- Generic instance reuse compared type-argument IDs with `!=`. A reference or
  slice type is freshly allocated per spelling, so two `Vec<&str>` uses
  produced two different nominal `Vec__Rstr` types and a TYPE-01 on any
  program passing one between functions. The cache now compares with `ty_eq`
  (exact for nominal types, structural for refs/slices). Found because the
  loader needs `Vec<&str>` in several functions. Regression
  `generic-instance-identity`.
- Indexing an array whose element is a fat type (`&str`, a slice) lowered as
  a *slice range* whenever the result type was `&str`/slice, so `a[1]` on an
  `[&str; N]` silently dropped the index and yielded the whole array (`.len()`
  read the array length). `ll_addr`/`ll_expr_index` now dispatch on the index
  node's shape (`a[lo..hi]` has a `..`), not the result type. Regression
  `array-of-fat-elements`.
- A generic parameter used as a *type argument* (`fn get<U>(h: Holder<U>)`)
  stopped with NAME-01 "no type named `U`": `ck_type_mentions_template_param`
  returned at a generic head without descending into its arguments, so
  inference tried to resolve `Holder<U>` while pinning `U`; and `ck_unify`
  treated a generic head with arguments as "nothing to pin", so `U` was never
  inferred. Both fixed. Regression `generic-param-as-type-arg`.
- `ck_ty_to_ast` spelled a monomorphized instance with its mangled display
  name (`Box__i32`), which is not a symbol; re-walking that type node (an
  inferred argument that is itself an instance, `wrap(inner)`) gave NAME-01
  "no type named `Box__i32`". It now rebuilds `Box<i32>` from the instance
  tables. Same regression.
- **Nested generic inference.** `ck_infer_generic_call` pins template
  parameters into a small global scratch table (`g_infer_targs`,
  `g_infer_names`, `g_infer_n`). Checking an argument recurses through
  `ck_expr`, so an inner generic call reset and filled the same table; when
  control returned, the outer call's slot already looked inferred and its own
  parameter was never unified. `wrap(wrap(42))` therefore built `wrap<i32>`
  for the outer call and rejected with "argument: expected `i32`, found
  `Box__i32`". The scratch table is now saved and restored around the
  recursive argument check. Regression `nested-generic-inference` (two to
  eight levels, multi-parameter generics, mixed depths, sibling arguments).
- **`methods` receiver arguments were dropped.** The parser parsed a methods
  header's `<...>` into `kids2` and then immediately overwrote that list with
  the function list; it also created an empty `kids` list, which made every
  `methods Box<...>` header look *generic*. So `methods Box<u8> { fn tag }`
  registered `tag` for every `Box<T>`: callable on a `Box<i32>`, running the
  `Box<u8>` body against it — a silent miscompile. harmony-base's node now
  has a third child list (`kids3`, matching the rewrite's `Node.kids3`), the
  header arguments live there, and `ck_register_methods` resolves them to one
  concrete receiver. Regression `method-wrong-instantiation` (error-handling)
  and `methods-instantiation`.
- **Several `methods` blocks on one base shadowed each other.** Generic method
  lookup matched only (receiver base name, method name), so
  `methods<T> Box<T> { fn get }` and `methods<T> Box<Box<T>> { fn get }`
  always resolved to whichever was registered first; `b.get()` on a
  `Box<Box<i32>>` got the shallow one and failed with a spurious TYPE-01.
  Lookup now scores each candidate by how specific its receiver pattern is
  and prefers the deepest match. Same regressions.
- **A block's own generic parameters were not bound.** `methods<U> Box<U>`
  substituted with the *type template's* parameter names, so `U` was left
  unresolved ("no type named `U`"); `methods<T> Box<Box<T>>` likewise failed
  to instantiate for a nested receiver. The block's own parameters are now
  unified from the concrete receiver before substitution.
- **Bare constructor in a method returning a nested instance.**
  `fn wrap(x: T) -> Box<Box<T>> { return Box { .v = Box { ... } } }` stamped
  the receiver's arguments on *both* bare `Box` literals, so the outer one
  became `Box<i32>` instead of `Box<Box<i32>>`. Auto-stamped arguments are now
  marked inferred (`ival == 1`) and yield to an expected type that names an
  instance of the same template.
- **Generic `&T` inference against `&str`.** `ck_unify` treated `&str` as a
  thin reference, so `fn f<T>(x: &T)` called with `&str` pinned `T` to the
  pair's byte element (`u8`) and silently compiled `f<u8>`. `&str` is a fat
  pair whose referent is unsized, so unification now leaves `T` unpinned and
  the call stops with "cannot infer". Regression
  `generic-ref-str-inference`. The remaining `&&str` collapse is a separate
  representation limit (§20.1), not an inference bug.
- **An `extern "C"` declaration in an imported module was namespaced.** The
  module loader renamed it (and calls to it) to `mod::lc_syscall3`, although
  the C ABI requires the raw symbol. The emitted `declare` was invalid IR.
  Extern declarations are no longer collected as module-level names; calls
  keep their raw spelling. Regression `imported-extern`.

The last two are mirrored in the rewrite checker (`harmony/sema/check.hr`), so
the type differential stays at 0 mismatches. The fuzzer's `specials` engine
also grew an `// EXPECT: <exit code>` oracle: opt-level agreement cannot see a
value that *every* level gets consistently wrong, which is exactly the shape
of the fat-element and `methods` bugs above.
